  "raw_text": "Step 1: Recognize that we need to compute $\\sin^3 18^\\circ + \\sin^2 18^\\circ$ directly without simplifying further unless necessary.\n\nStep 2: Factor out $\\sin^2 18^\\circ$ from the expression to get $\\sin^2 18^\\circ (\\sin 18^\\circ + 1)$.\n\nStep 3: Use the known value of $\\sin 18^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{5}-1}{4}$.\n\nStep 4: Substitute $\\sin 18^\\circ$ into the factored form: $\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}-1}{4}\\right)^2 \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}-1}{4} + 1\\right)$.\n\nStep 5: Simplify $\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}-1}{4}\\right)^2$ to $\\frac{6-2\\sqrt{5}}{16}$.\n\nStep 6: Simplify $\\frac{\\sqrt{5}-1}{4} + 1$ to $\\frac{\\sqrt{5}-1+4}{4} = \\frac{\\sqrt{5}+3}{4}$.\n\nStep 7: Multiply the simplified forms together: $\\frac{6-2\\sqrt{5}}{16} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}+3}{4} = \\frac{(6-2\\sqrt{5})(\\sqrt{5}+3)}{64}$.\n\nStep 8: Expand the numerator: $(6-2\\sqrt{5})(\\sqrt{5}+3) = 6\\sqrt{5} + 18 - 10 - 6\\sqrt{5} = 8$.\n\nStep 9: The expression simplifies to $\\frac{8}{64} = \\frac{1}{8}$.\n\nFinal answer: $\\boxed{\\frac{1}{8}}$<|im_end|>",
